INT. NIGHT TRAIN DINING CAR - MOVING

Candlelit tables, white cloths, silver trays. Rain streaks the black windows. The car sways.

VERA KOSTOVA, 40s, in a charcoal suit, stirs her tea with unnatural calm. Across the aisle, LENNOX BRADSHAW, 50s, drums fingers on his knife. Two tables away, DMITRI SOKOLOV, 30s, bulky in a fur-collared coat, studies the menu. A waitress carrying a tray: QUINN, 20s, red-haired, black apron, noticing everything.

She sets the tray down at Vera's table. Their eyes meet. Quinn straightens a napkin that doesn't need straightening.

QUINN
Anything else, ma'am?

VERA
Just the check.

LENNOX
The check, please.

QUINN
Of course, sir.

Neither woman moves. Dmitri closes the menu, slides one hand beneath the table. Vera's fingers tighten around the spoon.

The train hits a switch. The car lurches. A glass tips, shatters on the floor.

Everyone looks down, then up. Quinn is already gone. Dmitri lifts a silenced pistol onto the cloth. Lennox lifts a revolver from his coat. Vera lifts nothing, but she has risen, spoon in hand, between the two of them.

VERA
Gentlemen.

DMITRI ^
Vera.

LENNOX
Move, Vera.

VERA
Not a chance.

He fires. She falls sideways. The candle goes out.
